  {
    "episode_id": 51093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Well, here I think it means platforms that are so dominant that they have an incredible market share. They have a concentrated dominant market share in their respective markets. Take Google. Search. Google search. Everybody's familiar with Google search. We all probably use it. Google search accounts for something like over 90% of all searches done in, I think it's the world. It's a huge, huge number. Take Facebook, social media. of the American adults who use social media. And that's like 76% of adults in America use social media. 99% of them use Facebook. That is market power. That is concentrated market power. And you know it's a monopoly when you see them be able to kill competitors. And you see them be able to take stuff from us as Americans without our consent and we can't do anything about it. And we see both of those things. We see these companies buying up the competition we see them killing competitors like parlor for instance which they basically eliminated back in January with the snap of their fingers and you see them taking stuff from us our data our personal information our privacy and we can't do anything about it because there's no competition to go to to hold them accountable so that's that's monopoly these dominant platforms have become monopolies and it's time we broke them up.",
