  {
    "episode_id": 51093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "That's a great question. I think that what we need to do is to taking alphabet Google as the example. I think that Google should not be allowed to have dominant platforms across multiple industries. So you've got Google Search. Of course, they also have Google Cloud. And they also have Google Fiber. and they have any number of other Google-owned services. The Google, maybe the biggest one, or they also have a near monopoly, maybe an outright monopoly, is digital advertising. Google owns what's called the ad stack, which is the whole advertising apparatus. They shouldn't be allowed to own all this stuff. I mean, that's the simple answer. You shouldn't be able to have monopoly power in multiple industries at once. So I think one of the clearest things we can do is to pass legislation that says you've got to break these different lines up. You can't have this mass consolidation. These conglomerate mergers. They're sometimes called. And so I would say that Google ought to have to sell off Google Fiber and Google Cloud. I think that Facebook should have to sell off Instagram and WhatsApp. The Amazon shouldn't be able to own Amazon web services and also own the dominant e-commerce platform. So make these companies split off the various different industries that they have gathered together and allow there to be some real competition.",
