  {
    "episode_id": 51093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "So something we're trying to do here is give folks tools for thinking about this issue because the whole point of the 1970s comment is a lot of people on the right especially don't have a strong framework for thinking about this. So it's now conventional wisdom across the aisle that it was a mistake to approve the Facebook acquisition of Instagram in 2013. But what makes that hard if we take ourselves back in time is that the time Instagram was small. It had a strong valuation, but that doesn't really mean anything as long as they're not selling anything. So how should policymakers have thought of that acquisition back then? And how should that inform how they think of new acquisitions? So for example, when Twitter was talking about buying clubhouse a few months back.",
