  {
    "episode_id": 51093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "certainly for those of us who believe in the free market, that has been a traditional principle. And I think something that's been verified and ratified by economists. I mean, the more competition you have, the better it is, at least to productivity gains, at least to new investment, at least to innovation. I'm really concerned about the innovation slowdown in our economy across sectors. And I really do think that the concentration of market power in the tech space, but in other areas as well, has led and contributed to that innovation slowdown. Same thing with wages. There's multiple studies done that show that increased market power, market concentration is correlated with a declining share of GDP for labor. And that makes sense. If there's actually less competition, so to speak, for people's jobs, they've got fewer firms they can go to, then you have increasing power by the firms. And you see wages of flatlining, particularly for those who don't have a four-year college degree. So I think that competition is a pro-innovation, pro-productivity, pro-dynamism policy, and we need to be in favor of it. Take the Microsoft case from a couple of decades ago now, gosh, almost three decades ago, really. That case ultimately led to an avalanche, a wave of innovation in Silicon Valley, and in the same sector, the technology sector, partly because it freed up space there, free of space for competition. I think we need to do something similar in the tech space. The worst thing that we could do, is allow just a few dominant companies to basically freeze the technology space, to freeze out new startups, to freeze out new market entrants, all the while projecting their political power and trying to essentially run our government.",
