    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Oh, I would say it's not a catch-all. It's not the end-all of addressing ideological capture. It's a great word for it, but it is a big part of it. And I think that, listen, to have monopoly power, anytime monopolies gather this kind of power, anytime you see corporations become monopolies, I should say, they always want to project their power politically. We've seen that throughout our history. We saw it a century ago. You know, we had the railroads. What do they try to do? Buy entire state legislatures. They really had quite a bit of success with that. They tried to buy the United States Congress and it led to the biggest bribery scandal in American history in the Congress. We've seen this movie before and that's why it's not a total solution. It's not the perfect solution but it is a major step forward to say we're not going to allow these companies to control the economy, to squelch competition and to exert their power politically. And this is why trust-busting anti-monopoly policy, I think, is so, so important. One other point there, Sagar, just about these monopolies in different industries, it is true you could spin off AWS, and AWS could still be a monopoly in that particular sector. And, you know, our antitrust law should have something to say about that, too. Just because you limit the companies to one sector doesn't mean that, okay, now it's okay to be a monopoly, right? But I think we can start with allowing these huge companies to build conglomerates across multiple industries. Surely we don't want that. Surely we want to stop that and then we'll talk about the monopoly power in each industry.",
