  {
    "episode_id": 51093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think the narrative of monopoly, I think the facts are the facts. And if you look at the, again, the degree of market power and market concentration of these industries, I think it's real. I think that the facts there are just undeniable. Now, Google and Facebook can try to make the argument that, oh, well, our power is actually good. I mean, this is Facebook's latest pivot. They're China pivot, which is, oh, you need us to be a champion, an American champion, basically. Embrace us as a monopoly and we'll compete with China. My answer to that is that we don't give you monopoly status based on what you think you will do for a foreign nation or what you can do for us. That's not how it works in the United States. We like competition. Go compete. Go beat those Chinese companies, but do it competing with them, not because you've got monopoly status. American law and American antitrust enforcers are looking the other way. So I think the bottom line is, I don't think we should be trying to pick winners and losers in terms of, oh, I think this company ought to be, ought to do this. I think the outcome ought to be that. We should just be for competition. Let's keep competition robust. Let's encourage more innovation. Let's let the market change. We want new entrants. We want new competitors. We want it to shift. I don't want to be having this conversation with you about these same companies in three or four years. We want to be",
