  {
    "episode_id": 51093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and I'm not sure that we're in a position yet to say, and I would just say this. I think that we always want to err on the side of competition. We always wanted to err on the side of new market entrants, of new innovation. I think we should be pretty slow to say, well, this is basically a public utility. And now we've got to treat it as a public utility and regulate it like a public utility. I mean, sometimes there turns out to be no real option there. But I think we should always air on the side of actually let's promote competition, let's promote new market entry, let's promote new innovation. So I would be pretty slow, especially with technologies like these, to say, oh yeah, it turns out you have to be huge, you have to be a monopoly in order to succeed. Certainly if your view is if your business model is built around behavioral ads is built around algorithmic amplification as these platforms business model increasingly is then yeah of course they want to be huge because they need all the data they can get right they need massive amounts of data so they need to be gigantic they need to compete on a massive global scale. and they don't want competitors naturally. But this is another reason, Tucker, I think we ought to be careful and cautious about saying that, oh, yeah, that's a natural monopoly. Usually the folks who advocate for that, the loudest are the monopolists, right? They always say that. The monopolists always say, oh, hey, this is how it has to be. So just let us have this space and maybe we'll work out some kind of regulatory deal, which is basically what these companies are saying now. They're saying like, yeah, just regulate us a little bit, let us write the regulation and leave us alone. I think that what I would prefer to say is, hey, let's introduce some more competition. Let's break up your monopoly status and then let's see what the market does.",
