    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 51093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "Well, I would just say that the danger with getting cozy with monopoly power is that you can't have it both ways. I mean, you can't on the one hand say, yeah, these companies are monopolies and they're too powerful and we ought to decrease their influence in politics, their influence over the economy. But we do want them to censor more. That's basically what's been the democrat position. We want them to censor more and actually we kind of like their power over speech and in fact we wish they'd use it a little more efficiently is what the left is increasingly saying. I see my colleagues in on the left in the Senate. It's been interesting over the last two years. I've noticed a shift in their tone away from talk about how dangerous the concentrated power is and talking more and more about how the companies need to censor more. They use the phrases that you use in terms of, well, they need to be responsible with their curating. They need to not allow extremist voices. To me, this does get at a fundamental disagreement. I don't want there to be a big government, big business alliance. I'm against that. I don't want the government to be using companies as a cat's paw to carry out speech suppression that the government couldn't do because of the First Amendment. And this is another reason why I think the best policy here is to break up companies that have gotten this big is to disperse that power and introduce more competition. And I do think that's increasingly a pretty fundamental disagreement because the left would like to maintain the power over speech. They would like to maintain that kind of control. And my answer to them is you can't have it both ways. If you're going to let them have their monopoly power, You've got to then live with all of the other consequences of that, the economic consequences, the data consequences in terms of taking data, the loss of privacy, all of that goes with their monopoly status.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "It is inconsistent to criticize monopoly power while simultaneously demanding increased censorship from those same monopolistic firms.",
        "confidence": 0.98
      },
      {
        "text": "Monopoly power leads to negative consequences including economic harm, data exploitation, and loss of privacy.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "A coalition between big government and big business to suppress speech violates the First Amendment.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Breaking up dominant tech companies into smaller entities would reduce concentrated power and increase competition.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Some members of the left in the U.S. Senate have shifted focus from criticizing corporate power to emphasizing the need for greater content moderation.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The Democratic Party has advocated for increased censorship by large tech companies.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Large tech companies should be held accountable for curating content responsibly and suppressing extremist voices.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
