  {
    "episode_id": 51093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 45,
    "turn_text": "So the follow-up there is what does the evolution in Democrats rhetoric over the past two years mean for this broad project that you've engaged with? It's just what's been interesting as someone who's followed these debates of what are the pieces on you in 2018, 2019 where, wow, like the left and the right and everyone's coming together and this is so counterintuitive and, whoa, he doesn't like the Koch brothers, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera. But as things have shifted in a more partisan tone, as tech has become much more like, I think, Hollywood in the eyes of the right, it seems that this has just come into a culture where steelmate position. So to call back your earlier comment where you don't want us to be having the same conversation in three years, it seems like any issue that becomes a culture or issue basically goes into that direction unless the left is outright wins, like the gay marriage debate. So why should someone on the right be optimistic given the change in tone over the last two years?",
