    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 48,
    "turn_text": "Republicans changed so much? Because their voters are saying more and more clearly, we don't like these companies. We don't like being tracked. We don't like being censored. We don't like their power. We basically don't like anything. that they do in the way that they do it, and you need to hold them accountable. So I think that that is among voters. I think that's still a bipartisan position. And I think there's also a strain on the Democrat Party on the left. There is a strain of anti-monopoly, anti-statist tradition there that for them runs all the way back to the 19th century, all the way back to Andrew Jackson. That's a powerful strain in democratic politics. And I think that can be recovered. Right now, nobody's really speaking for it. Among elected officials, you don't hear that string being emphasized much. But you know, Franklin Roosevelt played on that string in the 1930s. He talked about, for a period, had an antitrust policy. He didn't end up doing much with it. But for a period, he pursued antitrust. He talked about the power of empowering the common man and woman, empowering the everyday work a day citizen and about the need to cut the trust down to size. That's there still. in the democratic tradition i think that that can be brought out and i think there's there's real opportunity for cross-partisan work on this but hey it's going to take a lot of effort and uh it's going to take shifting the debate and that's why i hope this book one of the reasons i go all the way back to the Gilded Age is I'm trying to challenge the paradigm of both right and left in a sense right now and I'm trying to break out of the stalemate by saying let's think a little differently here about what it looks like to give people control over their lives and maybe that's something that we could agree on together.",
