    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 50,
    "turn_text": "Oh, I think it can. I think it can. The question is going to be, will folks show some leadership and put forward substantive policies? And this is why people sometimes ask me. They say, well, listen, you're not in the majority right now. Why are you rolling out new antitrust proposals? Why are you rolling out new proposals on tech? Why are you continuing to do all this? You know that they're probably not going to get voted on anytime soon, at least not their current form. You don't have a majority and you don't have the White House. And my answer to that is, is really your question, Sagar. It's because we need to push forward. the ideas and the possibilities here. We need to show that look, there's different ways to think about this, that you can be absolutely pro-market, pro-competition, but anti-monopoly. Now, you can be for the workings of capitalism, but be against woke big business and against the idea of concentration of power by business across these different industries. To be honest with you, one of the reasons I wrote this book and went all the way back again to the Gilded Age was to try to show that both right and left, kind of got comfortable with monopoly power in the early 20th century and up through today. And I think we need to shake both right and left out of their complacency about this and say, we've got a real problem here.",
