  {
    "episode_id": 51093,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51093.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "I would say this that when you have, let's just take the approximate number of people in the last election who voted for the former president. You have 74, 75 million people. who voted for him and who aligned one can assume therefore with that point of view, that's a big market. And I think when you look at market power and you say, well, why are there not companies that are responding or catering to that market? Why does Major League Baseball, for instance, Senator Lee, my colleague Senator Lee raised this question recently. He said, why does Major League Baseball feel like they can take a position that is directly contrary and offensive to so many people who are their consumers And Mike's answer, Mike Lee's answer that was, it must be because they're a monopoly. I think that's correct. I think for many of these companies, they don't feel a need to go back to the big tech companies. They don't feel a need to directly compete for folks who have very different sensibilities than they do on these political issues, social issues, because they have monopoly status. They don't have any real competition. So to answer your question, will breaking up concentrated power, will that bring all sorts of new conservative alternatives into the marketplace, I don't know the answer to that. We can't control that. But I do think the concentrated power is dangerous always on its own terms, always. And we're seeing it vividly now and conservatives are awakening to it because these mega corporations have allied themselves with the left wing, woke, social policies and economic policies. The concentrated power is dangerous no matter what they're using it for. Just the idea that you would have a small group of people exerting outsize influence over government, over society, big, big problem. So I think that we have to address it. We've got to be able to open up the market for more competition and hope that that would give half of the country opportunity to find new competitors. But there's no guaranteed outcome on who knows what would happen. But what would certainly be good is breaking up this concentrated power can only be good for democracy. It can only be good for liberty. And I think that's what we need to be keyed on.",
